# board.env — Sengled Smart Hub G4 (E39-G8C), contributed by @hlyi
#
# STATUS: VALIDATED (#130). The NCP firmware built from this file was flashed to
# a real G4 and confirmed working end-to-end — Home Assistant talks to the radio
# (@hlyi, 2026-06-13). Every value below is a verified G4 hardware fact:
#
#   - BOARD_TARGET_DEVICE: MG13P ordering part number from the #133 hardware page.
#   - BOARD_UART_FLOW=sw: the G4 has no RTS/CTS wiring (#123), so it builds with
#     software XON/XOFF flow control (paired with the bridge's flow_control=sw).
#     This is a first-class SDK option, applied by the build — not a patch.
#   - BOARD_UART_PERIPHERAL / TX / RX: the G4 wires the EFR32 UART on the SAME
#     USART and pins as the Lidl reference (USART0, PA0 = TX, PA1 = RX), confirmed
#     on hardware by @hlyi in #130 ("G4 Radio IC also uses the same pin as Lidl").
#
# CTS/RTS entries are ignored when flow=sw, kept only so the file is uniform.
#
# Scope: the G4 now ships a prebuilt for every firmware — NCP @115200,
# OT-RCP @230400, RCP @230400, Z3 Router @115200, and the Gecko bootloader —
# so a G4 user flashes without a toolchain. Their validation histories differ
# and are documented per firmware in ../README.md: NCP was validated
# end-to-end on G4 hardware (#130), the bootloader on @hlyi's unit (#148), and
# 230400 is his measured operating point for this board (#134, #142). The RCP
# and Router images are builds of the same board facts that have not been run
# on a G4 — flash them knowing that, and keep a debugger within reach for the
# bootloader, whose bad flash is an SWD-only recovery. RCP: CPC has no
# software flow control, so flow=sw is built as none and recorded as none in
# radio.conf (the bridge — cpcd's TCP peer — is the chip's flow partner and
# must match).

BOARD_NAME="Sengled Smart Hub G4 (E39-G8C)"

# Exact MCU OPN handed to `slc generate --with` — verified from PR #133.
BOARD_TARGET_DEVICE="EFR32MG13P732F512IM32"

BOARD_UART_FLOW="sw"       # G4 has no RTS/CTS — software XON/XOFF flow (#123)

# --- Default baud for the serial-heavy firmwares (#134, #142) ---
#
# The project defaults (460800 for both) are the Lidl reference's, and they
# rely on RTS/CTS. This board has none, so the host's 16-byte RX FIFO must be
# drained within ~347 µs at 460800 — a stage no software flow control covers,
# which is where @hlyi's G4 measurements found the losses. At 230400 that
# budget doubles and the link is clean, so it is the operating point here and
# the baud whose prebuilts are committed. flash_efr32.sh reads these keys, so
# `BOARD=sengled-e39-g8c ./flash_efr32.sh -y otrcp` picks 230400 by itself.
#
# NCP (115200) and the Router (115200, its only baud) keep the project
# defaults, which are already below this ceiling.
BOARD_RCP_DEFAULT_BAUD="230400"
BOARD_OT_RCP_DEFAULT_BAUD="230400"

# --- Committed prebuilt matrix, read by make-all-bauds.sh ---
#
# Which bauds this board actually ships a prebuilt for. The reference board
# commits a wide NCP/RCP sweep because a Lidl user may pick any of them; here
# the point is the single operating point per firmware, so
# `BOARD=sengled-e39-g8c ./make-all-bauds.sh` rebuilds exactly the three
# committed G4 application images and nothing else. The RCP is not committed
# (never run on a G4): BOARD_RCP_BAUDS is empty on purpose, and a G4 user
# builds it with `BOARD=sengled-e39-g8c ./25-RCP-UART-HW/build_rcp.sh 230400`. The Gecko bootloader is
# absent from this matrix on every board — it carries no baud and is built in
# one shot by build_bootloader.sh.
BOARD_NCP_BAUDS="115200"
BOARD_RCP_BAUDS=""
BOARD_OT_RCP_BAUDS="230400"
BOARD_ROUTER_BAUDS="115200"

# --- UART routing: same as Lidl, confirmed on G4 hardware (#130) ---
BOARD_UART_PERIPHERAL="USART0"
BOARD_UART_PERIPHERAL_NO="0"
BOARD_UART_TX="A 0 0"      # PA0 = TX (G4 = Lidl, confirmed #130)
BOARD_UART_RX="A 1 0"      # PA1 = RX (G4 = Lidl, confirmed #130)
BOARD_UART_CTS="A 5 30"    # ignored when flow=sw
BOARD_UART_RTS="A 4 30"    # ignored when flow=sw

# --- Bootloader GPIO activation: "<port> <pin>" on the EFR32 (#148) ---
#
# The G4 wires an EFR32 pin to a host GPIO (RTL8196E line 13, declared as
# blmode-gpios in the board's devicetree, active-low open-drain). Holding it
# through an nRST pulse — the bridge's blmode_pulse — must land the radio in
# its bootloader, which only works if the bootloader samples the pin: hence
# the bootloader_gpio_activation component, added by build_bootloader.sh only
# for a board that sets this key.
#
# PB15, traced on the PCB by @hlyi (#148). Verified against the part: the G4's
# EFR32MG13P732F512IM32 is SDID 89, whose _GPIO_PORT_B_PIN_MASK is 0xF800
# (em_gpio.h), so pins 11-15 of port B exist — PB15 is a valid GPIO. It is
# also proof by construction: Sengled's own bootloader entered on this pin.
#
# The polarity is the SDK default (LOW = enter the bootloader when the pin is
# pulled down), which matches the host's active-low open-drain line: the
# bootloader configures the pin as an input with a pull-up, so it reads
# "inactive" whenever the host is not driving it.
#
# The Lidl reference board sets no such key — no EFR32 pin is wired to a SoC
# GPIO there (see POST-MORTEM-bootloader-recovery.md: not an oversight, the
# OEM engineered no hardware recovery path), so its bootloader keeps the
# component out and rebuilds byte-identical.
BOARD_BTL_ACTIVATION_PIN="B 15"

# Bootloader revision — see the same key in ../lidl/board.env for what this
# number is and why it is load-bearing. Short version: the bootloader accepts a
# stage-2 upgrade over UART only if the incoming image is strictly newer, and
# refuses in silence otherwise, having already erased the application (#148).
#
# 3, because this board's bootloader binary changed: it gained GPIO activation.
# A 2 here would be unreachable over UART on any G4 already running our earlier
# 2.4.2 build — which is exactly how #148 was found. Bump this on every change
# to the Sengled bootloader binary.
BOARD_BTL_CUSTOMER="3"
